    ($sg$ask-client$sg$, $sg$How do I ask a client something from a task?$sg$, $sg$Daily work$sg$, $sg$staff$sg$, $sg$When a task can't move until the client tells you something, sends a file or says yes, ask them from the task. The task waits on the client until they answer, and you see every open ask in one place.

### Ask without a task

From the Desk you can ask a client for something without picking a task. On the Desk's **Client** tab click **Ask client**, or click **+** in the top bar or the Desk rail and pick **Ask client…** (on a page that isn't a client's, pick the client first). It is the same window as on a task, with no task attached. The request shows under **Waiting on client** on the Client tab right away, and in **Slate › Waiting on client**.

### Ask from a task

1. Open a client task. **Client requests** sits above **Files and links** in the task details.
2. Click **Ask client**. It's there for a client task in **To do**, **In progress** or **Waiting on client**. A task in review or done says why it can't ask (take it back from review, or reopen it, first).
3. Pick what you need: **Question** (they type an answer), **Document** (they upload a file) or **Approval** (they say yes or no).
4. Add a short **Title** and a **Message** in plain words. They see both. For a question or approval you can tick **They should upload a file too**. **Needed by** is optional.
5. Under **Who should answer?**, tick one or more people: the client's portal users, or its **contacts** (people without a portal sign-in, like a treasurer, who answer through a link in the email). Up to 20 people.
6. Click **Ask**. The task moves to **Waiting on client**.

**Client request emails wait for the owner's switch.** Until the owner turns on **Ask client emails live** (**Settings → Firm settings → Emails**, **Client emails** card) and the client is in the pilot list, nothing is emailed: the toast says *Dry run: nothing sent* and who it would have gone to, so tell the client yourself and use **Record answer** when they reply. For a **test client** you get one copy marked **[Test]** instead, sent to you only, with a working link so you can try the whole thing. Once a client is switched on, the toast says how many people it went to.

### Add a client contact

In the Ask client window, click **+ Add contact**, then enter a name, an email and a role (Treasurer, Pastor, Board, Finance, Other) and click **Save contact**. It's ticked straight away. A contact belongs to that client only, and the same email can't be added twice. Contacts can't sign in to the portal; they only answer the request they were sent. To edit or remove contacts, use **Client contacts** in the client's tasks drawer. See [How do I add a client contact who answers by link?](#/help/client-contacts).

### How the client answers

- **Portal users** get an email with an **Open in the portal** button. It opens **Messages › Requests** in their portal, headed **What we need from you**, with your request at the top. They type an answer, attach files and click **Send**. Home also shows *Your bookkeeper is waiting on {n} items from you* with **Answer**.
- **Contacts** get a **Reply or upload** button that opens a page for just that request, with no sign-in. The link works for 14 days and for one answer.
- Reminder emails have a **Stop reminders** link for contacts. Clicking it pauses reminders for every open request for that client that lists them, even if the email covered several.

### Follow up

Each request in **Client requests** shows its status (**Waiting**, **No reply**, **Answered**, **Accepted**, **Cancelled**), who was asked, how many business days it has waited, how many reminders went out, and the answer once there is one.

- **Email again** sends the request again with a fresh link, and the link from the earlier email stops working.
- **Send reminder** sends a short reminder. Links already sent keep working.
- **Pause reminders** / **Resume reminders** stops or restarts automatic reminders for that request.
- **Turn off links** stops every answer link for that request (for example if it went to the wrong person). They can still answer in the portal.
- **Record answer** is for an answer that came by phone or email. It closes the request and adds the answer to the task's comments.
- **Cancel** (with an optional reason) withdraws a request you no longer need. Its links stop working.

After four reminders with no answer the chip says **No reply**. Call them.

### When they answer

The answer shows on the request and in the task's comments, marked *(client)* or *(by link)*. When the last open request on a task is answered or cancelled, the task goes back to where it was before. Then:

- **Accept** if the answer is what you needed.
- **Ask again** opens a new request with the same details if it isn't.

While a task has open requests it stays in **Waiting on client**. To move it on, record their answer or cancel the request.

### Waiting on client

**Slate › Waiting on client** (the hourglass in the Slate sidebar, `#/tasks/waiting`) lists every open request for the clients you work on, grouped by client with the longest wait first. **Just my tasks** shows only tasks you own. Click a request to open its task, or use **Send reminder**, **Email again** or **Cancel** right there.

### Document requests on the Documents tab

Document requests made from a client's **Documents** tab aren't linked to a task, so they don't show in **Client requests** or **Waiting on client**. Manage them on the Documents tab as before. The client sees them in **What we need from you** too, with an **Upload** button. See [How do I request a document from a client?](#/help/document-requests).
$sg$, array[$sg$ask client$sg$, $sg$ask the client$sg$, $sg$client request$sg$, $sg$client requests$sg$, $sg$question for the client$sg$, $sg$request approval$sg$, $sg$request a document from a task$sg$, $sg$waiting on client$sg$, $sg$waiting$sg$, $sg$#/tasks/waiting$sg$, $sg$send reminder$sg$, $sg$nudge$sg$, $sg$email again$sg$, $sg$resend$sg$, $sg$record answer$sg$, $sg$accept answer$sg$, $sg$ask again$sg$, $sg$cancel request$sg$, $sg$turn off links$sg$, $sg$answer link$sg$, $sg$client contacts$sg$, $sg$add contact$sg$, $sg$treasurer$sg$, $sg$pastor$sg$, $sg$dry run$sg$, $sg$nothing sent$sg$, $sg$client_request_enabled$sg$, $sg$what we need from you$sg$, $sg$messages requests$sg$, $sg$answer link$sg$, $sg$/respond$sg$, $sg$test copy$sg$, $sg$test client$sg$, $sg$stop reminders$sg$, $sg$ask without a task$sg$, $sg$ask client from the desk$sg$, $sg$+ menu$sg$]::text[], 114),
    ($sg$inbox$sg$, $sg$How do I message a client or a teammate?$sg$, $sg$Daily work$sg$, $sg$staff$sg$, $sg$The **Inbox** (in the left rail) holds client conversations and team chat in one place. The bell in the top bar also lists client messages waiting for a reply.

### Find a conversation

- The list on the left shows the people at your clients plus your teammates and groups.
- Use the **All / Clients / Team** buttons or the search box to narrow it down.
- **Waiting on you · 2h** means the client is waiting for a reply. A dot means unread.
- **+ New** starts a conversation with a client contact, a teammate, or a new group.

### Reply to a client

1. Open the conversation.
2. Make sure **Reply** is selected (not **Note**). The hint under it says who will see your message.
3. Type your message. **Enter** sends; **Shift+Enter** adds a new line.
4. Use the paperclip to attach a file (up to 25 MB). Client message attachments are saved in the firm's Google Drive, in the client's **Messages** folder for the year; an attachment on an internal note stays staff only. See [Where are client files stored?](#/help/google-drive-files).

### No reply needed

When the Desk is on and the client wrote last, the thread header has **No reply needed**. Use it when their message doesn't need an answer, like a thank-you: the thread leaves everyone's Desk and **Needs reply** count without sending anything, with **Undo** for a few seconds. A new message from the client puts it back. See [How do I use the Desk?](#/help/workbench).

### Thread actions: rename, pin, done, snooze, reassign, hide

Right-click a client thread in the list, or click its **⋯** (it shows when you point at or tab to the row, and always on a phone or tablet). With the keyboard, **Shift+F10** or the menu key opens it on the selected thread.

- **Rename…** gives the thread a name everyone on staff sees, like "2025 tax documents" (up to 80 characters). The person's name moves to the start of the preview line. **Clear name** puts it back.
- **Pin to top** keeps the thread at the top of **All** and **Clients**, newest pin first. **Unpin** undoes it.
- **Mark done** moves the thread out of **All** and **Clients** into the **Done** chip. **Reopen** brings it back, and so does a new message from the client.
- **Snooze** gives **Tomorrow 8am**, **Next Monday 8am** or **Pick a date…** (up to 30 days ahead), all Central time. The thread waits in the **Snoozed** chip with a **Snoozed until …** tag and comes back at that time, or sooner if the client writes. **Unsnooze** brings it back now.
- **Reassign…** picks who on staff owns the thread (anyone who can see the client), shown as an **Assigned:** tag. **Unassigned** clears it. Nobody is emailed.
- **Create task** makes a private task for you, "Reply to …", linked to the thread, and opens it.
- **Copy link** copies a link to the newest message, to paste to a teammate.
- **Hide** takes the thread out of the list for everyone. Only an admin can bring it back: admins see a **Hidden** chip and pick **Restore**. If the client writes again, the thread comes back on its own with a **Hidden** tag until an admin restores it.

**Pin**, **Mark done**, **Reopen** and **Snooze** (and **Hide**, for admins) show **Undo** in the message at the bottom for a few seconds.

Good to know:

- It is shared: what one person does, everyone on staff sees. The last change wins.
- The client never sees names, tags or any of these actions, and nothing is emailed. Each change is kept in an audit log.
- None of these mark messages read. The unread count, the dots and the bell still count unread messages in a hidden, done or snoozed thread.
- While you search, done and snoozed threads show too, with their tag (hidden ones only for admins).
- A link straight to a done, snoozed or hidden thread still opens it; the list switches to **All**.
- A client's **Messages** tab and the chat drawer have the same menu and tags, but nothing is hidden or moved there.
- On the Desk, right-click a message row, or open its **⋯** and pick **Thread actions…**, for **Rename…**, **Mark done** / **Reopen**, **Reassign…**, **Create task**, **Copy link** and **Hide** / **Restore**. There is no Snooze or Pin there (the Desk has its own **Snooze**), and the Desk does not hide or reorder rows by these. **Mark done** and **Hide** take the row off the Desk the way **No reply needed** does; **Reopen** (or **Restore**) puts it back once neither **Mark done** nor **Hide** still holds it, and never undoes a **No reply needed** someone set on the Desk. Thread names show only on the Inbox.
- Team chats have no menu, and it is off during **View as**.

### Who gets emailed when a client writes

Each client has an **account manager** (Jesse to start with), set on the Client overview under **Key dates and coverage**. When a client sends a message, only the account manager gets the "new message" email. If a client has no account manager, everyone with access to that client gets it, as before. Everyone with access can still see and answer the thread in the Inbox.

### Loop in the bookkeeper

The bookkeeper steps in when needed. Two ways to bring them in:

- Click **Loop in Marcus** (it shows the assigned bookkeeper's first name) next to **Reply** / **Note**. They get an email asking them to look at the conversation, and an internal note **"Looped in …"** is added to the thread so everyone on staff can see it. The client doesn't see it.
- Type **@** and their name in a reply or a note, for example **@Marcus** (first name, full name, or the part of their email before the @ all work). Sending it emails them. In a reply, the client sees the @name in the message, so use a note if you'd rather not.

The button doesn't show if the client has no assigned bookkeeper or you are the bookkeeper.

### Leave an internal note in a client thread

Switch to **Note** before typing (the hint says "Only staff see notes"), then click **Add note**. It's labeled **"Internal note · only staff see this"** in the thread. The client never sees it.

### Delete a message you sent

You can delete your own reply or internal note for **24 hours** after sending it. Click **Delete** under it, then **Delete** again to confirm (or **Cancel**). The message disappears completely, as if it was never sent: it's gone from the thread for you, your teammates and the client, and from the inbox preview, **Waiting on you**, unread dots and the bell. If the "new message" email hadn't gone out yet, it isn't sent.

- After 24 hours the **Delete** button goes away. You can't delete someone else's message, and you can't undo a delete.
- Clients can delete their own messages the same way, within 24 hours. If a client's message vanishes from a thread, they deleted it.
- A file attached to a deleted message is no longer shown in the thread.

### Details pane

Click the **i** button to open details on the right. From top to bottom:

- The client's milestone, cash on hand and this month's close progress.
- **Health**: the same status as the client picker (for example **Needs attention**) and the main reason. A status a staff member set by hand wins.
- **Plan**: Basic or Pro, and how many portal logins they have.
- **Bookkeeper** and **Account manager**. An **Out until {date}** tag means the bookkeeper is on [time off](#/help/time-off-coverage).
- **Writing**: who sent the thread, their role and whether they have full or limited access (or no portal login), plus the others at the client on the portal.
- **QuickBooks**: when it last synced. Click the pill to **Sync now**. A red line shows if the connection needs attention.
- **Waiting**: how many document requests are overdue, when they last wrote, and whether we've replied since.
- **Open for them**: open document requests (overdue ones say **Overdue** in red) and tasks.
- **Notes and SOP**: up to three pinned notes and the first two filled-in SOP sections, cut short. Open the client for the full text.
- **Recent portal visits**: the last few pages people at the client opened, and when.
- **Files in thread**, then **Request document**, **Add task** and **Open client**. **Open client** takes you to the tab you last used for that client, or the Overview.

Any section with nothing to show (or that you can't see) is left out.

### Chat from any page

The chat button at the bottom right (once the Desk is on, the messages icon on the Desk's rail on a computer), or **Create** → **Message** (top of the left nav; the round **+** in the top bar on a phone) on a client page, opens a small chat drawer without leaving the page you're on. Press **Esc** or click outside it to close.

### What the client sees on their Messages page

- Their conversation fills the page, with the newest message at the bottom and the message box underneath.
- Messages are grouped by day (**Today**, **Yesterday**, then the date). A gold **New messages** line marks where your unread replies start.
- Your reply appears on their page within a few seconds, without them refreshing. If they've scrolled up to read older messages, they get a **New message ↓** button instead of being jumped to the bottom.
- When they send, the message shows straight away with **Sending…** under it. If it doesn't go through it says **Not sent**, with **Retry** and **Edit** (Edit puts the text back in their message box).
- **Seen** appears under their latest message once someone on staff has opened that conversation in the Inbox.
- They can delete their own message for 24 hours after sending it (**Delete** under it, then **Delete** to confirm). It disappears for everyone, including you.
- The title says **Conversation with your MyGoodBooks team**, with the account manager and bookkeeper named under it.
- Each reply shows who wrote it: the name and **Title** from that person's **Settings → Profile**. With no title set, it says **Account manager** or **Bookkeeper** if that's their role for the client, otherwise just the name.
- The **Send** button stays grayed out until they type something or attach a file.

### Team chat

Team conversations support attachments and groups (**+ New group**). You can edit your own message within 15 minutes. You can delete your own message for 24 hours after sending it: click **Delete** under it, then **Delete** again to confirm (or **Cancel**). The message then disappears completely for everyone, as if it was never sent (no placeholder, and it drops out of previews, unread and **Seen**). After 24 hours the **Delete** button goes away. You can't undo a delete, and you can't delete someone else's message. A group you no longer need can be retired from the details pane: it drops out of everyone's inbox and becomes read-only, and any member can restore it later.

When an admin uses **View as**, the **Inbox** is private to that person: it shows a placeholder with links to the Messages tab of their clients. Messages from that side can't be sent, and team chat stays closed. See [How do I add staff and manage their access?](#/help/staff-management).

### Troubleshooting

- **"…these are sample threads and nothing you send is saved."** The portal couldn't reach client messages (or messaging isn't set up yet), so it's showing sample conversations. Refresh; if it continues, tell an admin.
- **A client says their Messages page shows "Couldn't load your messages."** Clients never see sample threads. If their messages can't load, they see this with a **Try again** button. Ask them to check their connection and click it; if it keeps happening, tell an admin.
- **A client says "Messaging isn't available right now."** The portal couldn't start messaging for them at all. Reply by email for now and tell an admin.
- **A client says a message shows "Not sent."** It wasn't saved. Ask them to click **Retry** (or **Edit**, then send again) once their connection is back.
- **A client can't find an older message.** Their Messages page shows the latest 200. **Load earlier messages** at the top brings in older ones. (They can press **Shift+Enter** for a new line; **Enter** sends.)
- **A client says a file link in their thread won't open.** Links refresh when clicked, so asking them to click it again usually works.
- **The bookkeeper says they didn't get a loop-in email.** Check their **Settings → Notifications**: loop-ins and @mentions follow "A client sends a message". Admins can check the **Emails** page.
- **"Loop in isn't set up yet."** A database update is missing. Tell an admin.
- **A message disappeared from a thread.** Its author deleted it within 24 hours of sending. Deleted messages can't be brought back.
- **"You can only delete a message within 24 hours of sending it."** It's past 24 hours, so the message stays. If it needs correcting, send a follow-up.
- **"Attachments are capped at 25 MB."** Send a smaller file, or share a Google Drive link instead.
