See [Slate basics](#/help/slate-basics). The Slate basics tour is in the ? menu.

### 2026-11-03: Lost clients
Admins can now mark a client **lost** when it stops being a client: **Manage access › Client organizations**, **Mark lost** on its row, then the first day it's no longer a client. From that day it leaves the **Clients** list, the client picker, pickers, alerts, the weekly digest and client health, its repeating tasks stop, and fee revenue stops on that date. Its history stays (under **Lost clients** at the bottom of the **Clients** page, or by typing its name in the picker), its QuickBooks Time hours still count in firm hours and pay cost, and nobody loses sign-in. **Undo lost** puts it back. See [How do I mark a client as lost?](#/help/lost-clients).

### 2026-11-02: HR › Members roster is tidier
- **One compact row per person**, in both **Admins** and **Bookkeepers**: photo (with a small status badge) and name with the email under it, **Role** with the **Active** tick under it, **Birthday**, **Start date**, **Clients**, **Access**, then **View as**, **Last seen**, **Offboard** and **Remove**.
- **Birthday** is a short month and a day side by side, with a small **×** to clear it.
- **Access** holds the temporary admin controls on bookkeeper rows and the owner's **MGB (internal)** tick on admin rows.
- The old unlabeled **×** at the end of a row now reads **Remove**. Nothing about who can do what has changed. See [Staff and access](#/help/staff-management).

### 2026-11-02: Slate is simpler to use
- **Mark done** is a big button at the top of an open task (or press **x**). It works like the circle on the row: review, recurring tasks and **Undo** all behave the same. See [What's in a task's details?](#/help/task-details).
- **One new-task button per screen.** Use **Create** (or **q**); the extra **+ Add** and **Add task** buttons are gone wherever **Create** is there. The **Add task** row at the bottom of each group stays.
- **A row click always opens the task.** On a task with subtasks, only the small arrow opens them. See [How do I use my Slate?](#/help/my-tasks).
- **Click someone's initials to reassign.** The two-arrow button is gone; with Slate plus, the person's profile is the first item in the menu.
- **The avatar menu holds the rest** in the Slate app: **My profile**, **Notifications**, **Display settings**, dark or light mode, the window sizes, **Dock in portal**, **Follow my client**, **Help**, **Admin settings** (admins) and **Sign out**. See [How do I use the Slate app?](#/help/tasks-own-window).
- New icons in Slate's sidebar so no two items look alike.

### 2026-11-02: Meet the Create button
The small **+** is now a **Create** button at the top of the left nav, under the logo (a round **+** in the top bar on a phone), with a roomier menu and an icon on each row: **Task** (press **q**), **Note**, **Request a document**, **Upload a file**, **Message** and **Start from a template**. With a client open everything is filled in for that client; with none open it adds a task. Admins also find **Add a client**, **Start a team review** and **Invite staff** under a small **Admin** heading; those only open the page where it is done. The Slate app's icon rail has its own **Create** with Slate's rows (**Task**, **New project**, **Recurring task**). The Desk rail's **+** is gone; **Create** replaces it. A short tour comes with it; rerun it from **?** → **New: Meet the Create button tour**. See [How do I add a task, note or document request, or message a client?](#/help/quick-actions).

### 2026-11-02: Average monthly hours on the Clients page
The **Clients** page has a new **6-mo avg** column: each client's average hours per month over the last 6 complete months, with a small bar chart. Click it to see each month against the budget. Admins also see a **6-month average** line on the client overview. See [How do I use the Clients page?](#/help/clients-page).

### 2026-10-31: The firm's clock-in time is no longer unmapped
Time logged in QuickBooks Time to **My Good Books (Clock-in Option)** is the firm's own time. It is now set up as such, so it no longer shows under **Unmapped** or in any client's hours on **Team › Hours**, and it doesn't appear on the QuickBooks mapping page as needing mapping. See [How do I connect the firm's QuickBooks Time and match customers and staff?](#/help/firm-quickbooks).

### 2026-10-31: Team › Hours shows hours only
The **Billable value** column is gone from the **Clients** table on **Team › Hours**, and from the Clients CSV. QB hours, averages, trends and hours budgets are unchanged. See [What's on the Team page?](#/help/team-page).

### 2026-10-31: Team › Hours is clearer about lag
Hours in the portal come from QuickBooks Online, which gets them from QuickBooks Time only when a pay period is approved, so the newest days can be missing. To stop that looking like zero hours: the **Capacity** card no longer has a **This week** column, its weekly average uses only the last 4 complete weeks that have fully synced, and **Avg / mo** on the Clients table uses only fully synced months. **Team › Hours** now says **Hours synced through** the newest day it has, and people paid monthly show **Paid monthly, hours post at month end**. A live connection to QuickBooks Time is not part of this. See [How do I check team capacity?](#/help/capacity).

### 2026-10-31: Clients can drop files anywhere
- **Clients drop a file on any page** of their portal (or tap **+ Add a file** on a phone), then pick a folder, a name, and who at their organization can see it: everyone, only some people, or everyone except some people. MyGoodBooks staff always see every file. Uploads show on the Desk's Inbox like any client file.
- **On a client's Documents page**, each file's **⋯** has **Rename**, **Who can see it**, **Replace with a new version**, **Copy link** and **History**. Tick several files to move them together, and a folder's **⋯** menu renames it. See [How do I add, file, hide or delete a client's documents?](#/help/add-documents).

### 2026-10-30: Organization tabs reach the client
Turning a tab off in **Client setup › Manage access › Organization tabs** now hides it for everyone at that client, on their own devices too (before, it only changed what your browser showed). Only admins can change these switches; other staff see them grayed out. If your browser had tabs turned off for a client the old way, they're saved for everyone the first time an admin opens Organization tabs for that client.

### 2026-10-28: Slate plus
A batch of additions to Slate, for all staff. Nothing you use went away; a few things moved. Take the **Slate plus tour** from **?** (about two minutes, it only looks). The full list is in [What's in Slate plus?](#/help/slate-plus).
- **One search.** The top-bar search (**Ctrl+K** / **⌘K**) finds people, clients, projects, tasks, SOPs and help in one place. On the Slate page, **Filter this Slate list** narrows your Slate instead; it replaces the search box next to **Add task**. The open client's transactions, documents and messages are no longer in this search.
- **Filters and saved filters.** A filter row on the Slate page (Assignee, Client, Project, Due, Status). Save a mix under **Saved filters › Save this view…** and set one as your default. The client menu in the toolbar moved into this row.
- **Calendar.** **Calendar** above a list shows its tasks by due date on a month grid; drag a task to another day to move it.
- **Icon sidebar.** Slate's sidebar is a slim row of icons in wide windows. Point at People, Projects or Clients for a quick menu, or click for the full panel with your pins first.
- **Profiles.** Click a person's name or badge to see their week, tasks and clients. The small **Reassign** button next to a badge changes who has the task. Make your own profile yours: status, local time, pins, goals, About me, favorites, out of office and section order.
- **Team load moved** to **Team › People**: open, due this week, late, Unassigned and the next 4 weeks. Admins make **Teams** there, and managers come from **HR › Employee records**.
- **Blocked by.** Make a task wait for another one; it sits in a **Blocked** group and its assignee gets **Ready to start** when it's free.
- **Custom fields.** Projects and client lists can have their own fields, like Fund or Grant (**Fields** in the list header).
- **Clearer @mentions.** The **@** list grays out anyone who can't see the task, and a line after you post says who was notified.
- **Hours against budget** next to a client's name and at the top of the **Assign** menu, amber at 80% and red at 100%.
- **Projects.** **New project** takes a description, client, due date, starter sections and people. Owners post status updates (On track, At risk, Off track, Done), and **Portfolio** shows every project's status in one table.
- **Rules (admins).** Small repeat steps on one project or client, with **Dry run** and **Recent runs**. Every rule starts off, and only the owner switches one on.
- Staff only: clients never see any of it, and it's not in **View as**.

### 2026-10-28: client agreements on Documents
- **An Agreements card on every client's Documents tab.** Keep the signed service agreement, engagement letter and any changes in one place, each with its type, status, signed and renewal dates, and an **Open** link to the Drive file. An **Expires in N days** pill shows when the renewal date is within 60 days.
- **Who can change them.** Admins and the client's account manager add, edit, **Mark signed**, show or hide, and move agreements to the trash; **Paste several links** adds a batch of old signed documents at once. Bookkeepers can read them. Only an admin can restore from the trash.
- **Clients don't see them yet.** New agreements start **Staff only**, and clients see nothing until the owner turns client agreements on. The staff note is never shown to clients. See [Client agreements](#/help/client-agreements).

### 2026-10-28: Slate app size buttons come back

In the Slate app, if clicking **Sliver**, **List** or **Full page** did not resize the window, the size buttons used to disappear for a while. Now they come back as soon as you drag the window larger.

### 2026-10-27: search your Slate list
- **A search box on the Slate page.** Next to **Add task**, type part of a task's title, its client's name or a word from its notes, and the list narrows as you type. Every word you type has to match. Groups, counts and the board show only the matches. **Esc** or the **×** clears it. It's in the Slate app's list too; the **Search tasks** box at the top of the Slate app still searches every task you can see. See [How do I use my Slate?](#/help/my-tasks).

### 2026-10-27: small fixes
- **Custom repeats keep their schedule.** Opening a repeating task's **Repeat** menu and saving again no longer shifts the schedule when the due date had moved off a weekend or holiday. It still counts from the first date.
- **Wording.** The profile prompt, **Settings › Profile** and the celebrations article now say admins celebrate with you, since celebrations are admin only for now. The client profile form now says **Founding date** and "celebrate your anniversaries" (the Staff card matches), and its fiscal year end day list fits the month.
- **Smaller fixes.** The Inbox uses your local date for "overdue", the Team upcoming card loads when it becomes visible, and a client signing in with the emailed code now lands in their portal without a reload.

### 2026-10-27: Work › Slate is now a short summary
- **One full Slate.** The Slate page is now the only full Slate. **Work › Slate** shows a short summary instead: what's **Late**, what's **Due today**, and your **Next** five tasks, with an **Open Slate** button. Click a row to open the task, just as you would on the Slate page; if there are more, a line says so and links to Slate.
- **Nothing else moved.** Notes & SOPs, Close, Deadlines and Ledger review are unchanged. The Desk keeps its compact **Slate** tab, now with an **Open in Slate** link. With the Slate app switch off, **Work › Slate** is unchanged. See [What's on the Work page?](#/help/work-page).

### 2026-10-27: celebrations are for admins only (for now)
- **Birthdays and work anniversaries are admin only.** Only admins see them now, 30 days ahead on **Today**, in **Team › People › Celebrations** and in the weekly digest. Bookkeepers, temporary admins and account managers no longer see celebration lines, the Celebrations card or a celebration shout-out button, and the short-lived 7-day heads-up for them is withdrawn. Shout-outs themselves are unchanged: anyone can still thank a teammate from **Team › People › Shout-outs** or a profile. See [Birthdays, work anniversaries and gift reminders](#/help/celebrations).

### 2026-10-27: client gifts follow the founding date (admins)
- **Founding date is now the default.** On **Team › Client gifts**, **Anniversary to follow** now starts on the **Founding date**, the client's own birthday, instead of **Client since**. A client with no founding date yet (blank or **Not sure**) still shows on their Client since date, so nobody drops off. Admins can switch back to **Client since** at any time. **Client since** stays on file and now has its own column in the list, and the Organization profile card on a client's Overview still shows both dates. See [How client gifts work](#/help/client-org-profile).

### 2026-10-27: celebrations a month ahead (admins)
- **Time to plan gifts.** Admins now see staff birthdays and work anniversaries **30 days ahead** (it was 7) on **Today** (the **This week** card, with **Gift sent** and **Dismiss** under each line), in **Team › People › Celebrations**, and in the weekly digest's **Coming up** section (it was 14 days). Each line gives the date, how many days away, and years on the team, for example "Debra Ruffin's birthday, May 27 · in 30 days · 3 years on the team". No birth year or age is shown. Client anniversaries already looked 30 days ahead in **Team › Client gifts** and the digest. Nothing changes for non-admins. See [Birthdays, work anniversaries and gift reminders](#/help/celebrations).

### 2026-10-27: birthdays are just a month and day
- **No more birth year.** **Settings › Profile** and **HR › Members** now ask for the **month** and **day** of a birthday, nothing else. The portal never shows or works out an age, and a year entered earlier is no longer shown anywhere. Celebrations, Today and the weekly digest work the same as before. See [Birthdays, work anniversaries and gift reminders](#/help/celebrations).

### 2026-10-26: Slate, start to finish tour and Slate settings
- **A tour of all of Slate.** **?** → **Slate, start to finish** walks through Slate in seven short chapters: your day, client lists, working a task, speed, team flow, automation and your setup. It uses big cards you can present to the team, a **Chapters** list to jump around and an **Exit** button. The tour only looks: it never changes a task. On a computer it runs in the Slate app.
- **Slate settings.** The **gear** in the Slate app opens **Settings**: how Slate opens, which window **Open Slate app** uses, row density, quick keys, how far **Undo** goes and where your task notifications are set. It's just for you, in this browser. In the portal, **?** → **Slate settings** on a Slate page opens it.
- **Replay it any time.** The Slate app's avatar menu has **Take the Slate tour**, and **Settings › Help** has **Replay the Slate tour**.

See [How do I take the Slate tour?](#/help/slate-tour) and [How do I change my Slate settings?](#/help/slate-settings).

### 2026-10-26: Slate is now its own app

- **Open Slate app.** On the Slate page and on the Desk's **Slate** tab, **Open Slate app** opens Slate in a window of its own. It replaces **Open in own window**. The portal never opens it for you, and Slate inside the portal works as before.
- **Install it.** **Install Slate app** adds Slate to your Dock or Start menu in Chrome or Edge on a computer, as a second app next to MyGoodBooks.
- **Four sizes.** Shrink the window to a **sliver** with just your current task and **Start** and **Done**, a **compact** list, a **regular** list with a rail, or a **full** page with a detail pane. The **Sliver**, **List** and **Full page** buttons jump to a size.
- **Shape your work.** In the app: named **Views** you can save and switch, bulk **Status**, **Priority** and **Section**, **Manual order** by dragging rows (or Alt+Up and Alt+Down), and a **List | Board** switch with columns by status or section.
- **Quick actions.** In the app: a right-click (or press and hold) menu on every task, **Cmd+D** to duplicate, **Cmd+C** and **Cmd+V** to copy and paste, drag a box to select several, **E**, **Enter** and **X** on the focused task, drag a task onto a date or a person, swipe right to finish and left to snooze on a touch screen, a two-finger swipe back, and a 9-minute **Undo**.
- **Links open in the portal.** A client page, chat or file opens in your portal window and the app stays put.

See [How do I use the Slate app?](#/help/tasks-own-window) and [How do I install MyGoodBooks as an app?](#/help/get-the-app).

### 2026-10-26: Month rhythm tasks are on

- **Monthly tasks for every client.** Slate makes monthly **Check in**, **Send reports** and **Follow up** tasks for every client, due on the firm's month rhythm dates (the 4th, 14th and 24th unless an admin changed them in **Settings › Firm settings › Month rhythm**). They start from October 25, 2026; earlier months are not filled in.
- **Client emails, ready to copy.** Check in, Send reports and Follow up tasks come with a draft client email built from the client's data. Open the task and choose **Draft email**, or click the envelope in a list. Nothing is sent: you copy it into your own email.
- **Full or Preliminary.** Finishing a Send reports task asks whether the reports went out full or preliminary, and lists what is still missing for the client.
- **Waiting on a client is never late.** A task waiting on a client, or a monthly task in a month whose reports went out preliminary, never counts as late: not on Today, the Team page, Slate's Overdue group, on-time stats or the performance score.
- **Close order.** A client's list has a new **Month view**: **The thirds** groups by part of the month, **Close order** shows each month's steps in order with a **Next** pill.
- **Admins:** each client can have its own three days (**Client setup › Access and details › Month rhythm**), and **Pre-fill Quarterly and Annual work by client type** on **Slate › Recurring** makes draft templates for churches, nonprofits and for-profit clients.

See [How do recurring tasks from templates work?](#/help/task-templates), [What's in a task's details?](#/help/task-details) and [How do client task lists and projects work?](#/help/task-lists-and-projects).

### 2026-10-25: Custom repeats in Slate
- **A Repeat menu like Asana's.** In a task, **Repeat** → **Custom…** lets you repeat daily, weekly on chosen weekdays, monthly (a day, the 2nd Tuesday, or the last day), quarterly, yearly, or every 2 weeks, every 3 months and so on. Add a time of day, skip weekends and firm holidays, and set an end (never, on a date, or after some times). It lists the next five dates before you save.
- **Recurring templates** have a new **Custom schedule** option with the same menu (date only, no time of day). The old choices work as before.

### 2026-10-25: Customize button has a new icon

- **Sliders icon.** The **Customize** button at the bottom of the left sidebar now shows a sliders icon instead of a gear, so it is not mistaken for Settings. It works the same.

### 2026-10-25: Slate app: Back, Forward, Refresh and deeper Undo
- **Back, Forward and Refresh.** The Slate app window and the installed MyGoodBooks app have no browser buttons, so three round buttons now sit in the top bar (beside the Slate logo in the Slate app). In a normal browser tab they stay hidden; use the browser's own.
- **Undo goes back 5 steps.** In the Slate app, **Cmd+Z** (Ctrl+Z) undoes your last 5 changes, newest first, each for 9 minutes. A change someone edited since is still left alone and says so.

### 2026-10-25: Client organization profiles and anniversary gifts

- **Clients fill in a short profile.** When they sign in, clients are asked for the name for cards, organization type, established date, gift address, who receives deliveries, a main contact and their fiscal year end. **Later** puts it off until the next sign-in. Optional get-to-know questions too.
- **Organization profile card.** On a client's Overview, under Key dates: their answers, a note when their fiscal year end differs from Key dates (Key dates is not changed), and **Client since**, which you can set by hand.
- **Onboarding step.** **Client finished organization profile** ticks itself.
- **Team › Client gifts (admins).** Anniversaries in the next 30 days with **Plan**, **Mark sent** and **Skip**, past gifts, and the line the weekly digest shows. **Anniversary to follow** picks the **Founding date** (the default) or **Client since** for the whole firm. Nothing emails clients or orders gifts.

See [What is a client's organization profile, and how do client gifts work?](#/help/client-org-profile).

### 2026-10-23: View as covers the whole app, and Last seen (admins)

- **View as covers the whole app.** On **HR › Members**, **View as** now shows a bookkeeper's Today (in its classic form), Clients and each client's pages, Work (with a read-only snapshot of their Slate), Team and HR. Their Inbox, Reviews and Handbook show a private placeholder, and private tasks stay hidden. See [How do I add staff and manage their access?](#/help/staff-management).
- **It's read-only.** The bar says "Read-only. Nothing you do here is saved." Anything that would save or send is refused with a note, and each blocked attempt is recorded in the [Audit log](#/help/audit-log) as *View as: blocked change*. Budgets show **Approve** grayed out, with a link to exit View as and approve.
- **Last seen.** A new **Last seen** button next to **View as** shows a person's last page and time, device, browser, app version, last sign-in and last error, with a link to **Recent errors**. Admins only. See [Usage stats and Developer tools](#/help/admin-insight).
- **Usage stats ignore View as.** Time you spend in View as no longer counts as the bookkeeper's activity.

### 2026-10-22: Change a client's bookkeeper from its list (admins)

On a client's Slate list, admins can click the **Bookkeeper** in the header, pick someone else and click **Reassign**. A ticked box also moves the old bookkeeper's open tasks on that client to the new one; tasks in review, private tasks and tasks anyone else owns or is on stay put. No email is sent. See [Change a client's bookkeeper](#/help/task-lists-and-projects).

### 2026-10-21: The Desk, a left sidebar and a new look

The Desk and the new **Today** are on for everyone, with a **left sidebar** under the rail (your **Pinned** and **Recent** clients, and a sliders-icon button to **Customize** it), the **Lato** font, brand colors (navy and teal), and menus that open when you point at them. Press **D** to open the Desk. A short tour covers all of it: **?** → **New: The Desk, a left sidebar and a new look**. The old Today is one click away in the **?** on the **Do next** card. Admins and temporary admins can switch the old fonts, colors and menus back on this computer under **Developer tools › Feature flags**. See [What changed: the Desk, left sidebar and new look](#/help/new-look-and-sidebar).

### 2026-10-21: Ledger review: Roll-forward

Ledger review has a new **Roll-forward** panel under **Reconciliation**. For each bank, credit card and loan account it shows the month's beginning balance, the money in and out by type, and the ending balance, then checks that ending against QuickBooks. An account that doesn't tie opens by itself and shows the difference. It's for looking only; no flags are created. See [How does Ledger review work?](#/help/ledger-review).

### 2026-10-21: Reviews: see the comparison together in your meeting

Once both sides of a quarterly review are in, the comparison now opens first for your reviewer and the admins, so they can prepare. You get an email and a **Both sides complete. Set up a time with** (your reviewer) message on **My review**. In the meeting, your reviewer (or Jesse or Bailley) clicks **Reveal comparison**, and you see it together. Signing opens after the reveal. Reviewers see **Ready to reveal** on **Reviews I'm giving**. See [How do quarterly reviews work?](#/help/quarterly-reviews) and, for admins, [How do I run a review cycle?](#/help/review-cycles).

### 2026-10-21: No more photo popup

The full-page **Add your profile photo** screen is gone. Everyone has a task, **Finish setting up your portal profile**, due Wednesday, October 28. Add your photo under **Settings › Profile** when you have a minute. See [How do I add or change my profile photo?](#/help/profile-photo).

### 2026-10-21: Month rhythm setting (admins)

Admins have a new **Month rhythm** card in **Settings › Firm settings**. It holds the three days each month when Slate's monthly tasks will come due: **Check in** (4th), **Send reports** (14th) and **Follow up** (24th), plus what happens when a date falls on a weekend or firm holiday. For now it only stores the dates; the monthly rhythm tasks come later, so nothing changes for anyone else yet. See [How do I change my settings?](#/help/staff-settings).

### 2026-10-21: Group, sort and filter Slate your way

Slate has a new bar above the list. **Group by** now offers **Due date**, **Client**, **4/14/24th** (Check in, Send reports, Follow up) and **Status**. **Sort** chips order tasks inside each group by due date, priority, status or name. Two quick chips, **Mine today** and **Waiting on client**, narrow the list in one click, and **Reset view** puts it back. Your view is remembered per person. The same bar is on the Desk's **Slate** tab. A short tour comes with it; rerun it from **?** → **New: Group, sort and filter Slate tour**. See [How do I use my Slate?](#/help/my-tasks).

### 2026-10-20: Handbook approver changes and a "sent back" email

- **Only the Co-CEOs change the approver.** On the Handbook tab, only Bailley and Jeff see **Change** next to the policy approver. Other admins still see who it is, with the note **Only Bailley or Jeff can change the approver.**
- **Sent back by email.** When the approver clicks **Request changes**, whoever edited the draft last gets one email with the note and a link to the draft. Nobody is emailed if the approver made the last edit. Switch it off in **Settings › Notifications › Handbook drafts sent back to me**. See [How do I sign the Staff Handbook?](#/help/staff-handbook).

### 2026-10-20: Anonymous survey answers show right away

Admins now see the anonymous answers to survey questions 7 and 10 as soon as one person has answered, including for the current quarter. They still show without names. The survey page tells staff that, with a small team, an admin may be able to guess who wrote one.

### 2026-10-19: Handbook drafts need approval

- **One approver.** A handbook draft can only be published once the policy approver (Bailley) has approved its current text. Each draft shows **Awaiting Bailley's approval**, **Changes asked** or **Approved**, and the Drafts group has filters with counts. Published policies, including the Flexible Work policy, don't change.
- **For Bailley.** **Needs your approval (N)** on the Handbook tab, a Today row, the count on the tab and at most one email every 30 minutes (switch it off in **Settings › Notifications › Handbook drafts to approve**). In a draft, **Approve version N** or **Request changes** with a note.
- **Admins: edit any draft.** **Edit text** (Markdown with a live preview) and **Edit details** work on every draft, approved or not. Saving new text sends it back to Bailley, and the editor warns you first. To change a published reference policy, use **Publish new version** (always a minor edit). **Change** at the top of the tab picks a different approver.
- **The Employee Handbook, chapters 1 to 9,** are loaded as reference drafts waiting for approval, with **[TBD: ...]** where details still need filling in. See [How do I sign the Staff Handbook?](#/help/staff-handbook).

### 2026-10-19: Month swing in Ledger review

- **A new low priority check.** **Month swing** flags an income or expense account whose month total is at least $1,000 and at least 50% away from its average over the three months before it, up or down, with the numbers in the flag. It needs at least two earlier months in the pull, so the usual 14-month pull shows it and a year-to-date pull early in the year may not. See [How does Ledger review work?](#/help/ledger-review).

### 2026-10-19: HR is now its own place

- **A new place in the left rail.** **HR** sits right below **Team**. **Handbook**, **Reviews** and **Onboarding** moved there, and admins also find **Members** (real admins, **Employee records**) there. **Team** keeps **People**, **Performance** and **Inventory**, plus **Hours**, **Reply times**, **Feedback** and **Ledger review** for admins.
- **Old links still work.** A bookmark or email that points at `#/team/handbook`, `#/team/reviews`, `#/team/onboarding`, `#/team/employees` or `#/team/members` opens the same page under HR.
- **One number.** The number next to HR in the rail adds up what's waiting on you under **Reviews** and **Handbook**. A short tour comes with it; rerun it from **?** → **New: HR is now its own place tour**. See [What's on the Team page?](#/help/team-page).

### 2026-10-19: Reference policies and employee records

- **Two kinds of handbook policy.** **Team › Handbook** now has **Signed** policies (bookkeepers sign, as before) and **Reference** policies (read only; nobody signs them, and they never add a Today row, a badge or an email). The list starts with **Policies to sign**, then the reference policies by section, with a search box. See [How do I sign the Staff Handbook?](#/help/staff-handbook).
- **Admins: New policy.** Add a policy from the Handbook tab. It's saved as a reference draft only admins see. A new policy never asks anyone to sign; **Make this a signed policy** is the one step that does, and only after you confirm it.
